.Dd $Mdocdate: August 2 2026 $
.Dt FuguLib::Privdrop 3p
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm FuguLib::Privdrop
.Nd permanent drop of root privileges
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Bd -literal
use FuguLib::Privdrop;

FuguLib::Privdrop->prepare_statedir(
    path => '/var/run/myapp',
    user => '_myapp',
);
FuguLib::Privdrop->drop_privileges(user => '_myapp');
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
does the privilege drop that an
.Ox
daemon does when its privileged work is complete.
The daemon binds the reserved port and prepares the state it must own.
Then the daemon gives up root permanently and runs the event loop as an
unprivileged user.
.Pp
The module keeps no state and has two class methods.
.Ss prepare_statedir
.Fn prepare_statedir "%args"
creates the state directory when it is absent, sets its mode, and gives
it and the files inside to the unprivileged user.
Root runs this before the drop.
.Pp
The create step is not a first-install special case.
.Ox
clears
.Pa /var/run
at every boot, so a daemon with a state directory there finds it absent
on each start.
.Pp
These are the arguments:
.Bl -tag -width "on_warnXX"
.It Fa path
The state directory.
This argument is necessary.
.It Fa user
The name of the user that owns the directory after the call.
This argument is necessary.
.It Fa group
The name of the group.
If you omit it, the method uses the user's primary group.
.It Fa mode
The directory mode.
The default is 0700.
.It Fa on_warn
A code reference that the method calls with a message for each problem.
A file inside the directory that stays unchanged is a warning, not a
failure: the daemon may never need that file.
.El
.Ss drop_privileges
.Fn drop_privileges "%args"
switches the process to the given user and group.
It then makes sure that the process cannot get root again.
.Pp
These are the arguments:
.Bl -tag -width "keep_groupsXX"
.It Fa user
The name of the user to become.
This argument is necessary.
.It Fa group
The name of the group to become.
This argument is optional.
If you omit it, the method uses the user's primary group.
.It Fa keep_groups
Keep the supplementary groups that the process inherits from root.
The default is 0, which reduces the group list to the one group.
On
.Ox ,
a value of 1 is how a daemon keeps access to the
.Xr mdnsd 8
socket after it drops to its own user.
.El
.Pp
If the effective user ID is not 0,
the method returns immediately.
Thus a program that is already unprivileged can call it in all cases.
In the other case, the method resolves the user and the group.
It calls
.Xr setgid 2 ,
then sets the supplementary groups with
.Xr setgroups 2 ,
then calls
.Xr setuid 2 ,
and sets the real and effective IDs.
Then it makes sure that the two IDs are not 0.
It also makes sure that a call to
.Xr setuid 2
with 0 does not bring root back.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn prepare_statedir
returns 1 on success.
It returns
.Dv undef
when the directory itself is not usable.
.Pp
.Fn drop_privileges
returns 1 on success.
A call from a process that is already unprivileged is a success.
The method never returns on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
This example binds a reserved port as root and then runs as
.Ar _myapp :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
my $socket = IO::Socket::INET->new(
    LocalPort => 80,
    ReuseAddr => 1,
    Listen    => SOMAXCONN,
) or die "Cannot bind port 80: $!";

FuguLib::Privdrop->prepare_statedir(
    path    => '/var/db/myapp',
    user    => '_myapp',
    on_warn => sub ($msg) { $log->warning('%s', $msg) },
);
FuguLib::Privdrop->drop_privileges(user => '_myapp');

while (my $client = $socket->accept) {
    handle_client($client);
}
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
.Fn drop_privileges
does not return a failure code.
It dies in these conditions:
.Bl -bullet -offset indent
.It
The
.Fa user
argument is absent.
.It
The method cannot resolve the user or the group.
.It
.Xr setgid 2
or
.Xr setuid 2
fails.
.It
The process holds root after the calls.
.It
A later
.Xr setuid 2
with 0 brings root back.
.El
.Pp
The caller must not continue after a privilege drop that is not
complete.
The re-escalation check therefore runs outside an
.Ic eval :
a swallowed failure would report a successful drop for a process that
kept root.
.Pp
.Fn prepare_statedir
dies only when an argument is absent or a name does not resolve.
Every other problem goes to
.Fa on_warn .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr setgid 2 ,
.Xr setgroups 2 ,
.Xr setuid 2 ,
.Xr FuguLib::Daemon 3p ,
.Xr FuguLib::Process 3p
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Dick Olsson Aq Mt hi@senzilla.io
.Sh CAVEATS
.Fn prepare_statedir
does not walk into subdirectories.
It changes the owner of the directory and of the files one level inside
it.
.Pp
A daemon that must keep a root-owned file, for example a PID file in
.Pa /var/run ,
must not put that file inside the prepared directory.
